LEARN.add({"subject":"ai","lessons":[
{
"id":"ai-d2",
"subject":"ai",
"level":3,
"order":14,
"type":"deep",
"minutes":60,
"title":"Design your AI-powered workflow",
"hook":"Map one real week, find the hours AI can give back, and build the controls that keep client data safe.",
"tags":["ai workflow","automation","prompt library","data privacy","reconciliation","review","productivity","accounting"],
"blocks":[
{"t":"chapter","title":"Map the week before you change it","minutes":6},
{"t":"p","text":"Six months after the prompting workshop, Elena is good at prompts. She writes clear briefs, tests formulas, and saves templates. Yet her week still feels as full as ever. The reason is simple: she uses AI the way most people do, one task at a time, whenever she remembers. Good prompts on random tasks give random savings."},
{"t":"p","text":"This masterclass is about the next step: designing a **workflow**. Instead of asking “can AI help with this email?”, Elena asks “which parts of my whole week should AI touch, how, with what data, and who checks the result?” Over eight chapters we will rebuild her week, with privacy controls strong enough to show a partner, a client or an auditor."},
{"t":"term","word":"AI workflow","def":"A repeatable sequence for a recurring task that says where AI helps, what data it may see, which template it uses, and where a human reviews before anything leaves your hands."},
{"t":"h","text":"Step one: a plain time log"},
{"t":"p","text":"You cannot improve what you have not measured. Before touching any tool, Elena keeps a simple log for one normal week. Every time she switches task, she writes the time and a two-word label in a note on her phone: “9:10 Rec B”, “9:55 Email F”. It feels tedious for two days, then becomes automatic."},
{"t":"p","text":"Her week is typical for a senior accountant at a mid-size firm. She serves four clients: Client B, a restaurant group; Client D, a wholesale distributor; Client E, a freelance designer; and Client F, the US subsidiary of an Italian manufacturer. She also reviews three juniors, J., M. and S."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"bars","data":[{"label":"Reconciliations","value":9,"highlight":true},{"label":"Client emails","value":8,"highlight":true},{"label":"Reviewing juniors","value":7,"highlight":true},{"label":"Memos","value":6,"highlight":true},{"label":"Close entries","value":6,"highlight":false},{"label":"Meetings","value":6,"highlight":false},{"label":"Research","value":5,"highlight":true},{"label":"Admin","value":3,"highlight":false}],"unit":"","caption":"Elena’s logged week in hours: 50 in total, five areas highlighted for redesign."},
{"t":"p","text":"Two things stand out. First, the highlighted areas take 35 of her 50 hours, and all five are heavy with words, patterns or checking, which is where AI is strong. Second, meetings and close entries are mostly about judgment, relationships and posting to the books, where AI has a smaller role."},
{"t":"h","text":"Break tasks into steps"},
{"t":"p","text":"A label like “reconciliation” hides many smaller steps. AI rarely replaces a whole task, but it can take over specific steps inside it. So for each highlighted area, Elena writes the steps in order, the way she would explain them to a new junior."},
{"t":"example","title":"Decomposing one task: Client D bank reconciliation","text":"1. Download the bank statement and the general ledger cash detail (10 min)\n2. Line up both lists in a spreadsheet (15 min)\n3. Match items, flag the unmatched ones (35 min)\n4. Investigate each unmatched item (25 min)\n5. Write explanations for the reconciling items (15 min)\n6. Tie out totals and sign off (10 min)\n\nTotal: about 110 minutes. Steps 3 and 5 are pattern and language work, the classic AI zone. Step 4 needs the client’s documents and her judgment. Step 6 is the control that must stay human."},
{"t":"p","text":"This is the key mental shift of the masterclass. You are not asking whether AI can “do reconciliations”. You are asking which _steps_ it can do faster, while the steps that carry responsibility stay with you."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"stat","items":[{"value":"50 hrs","label":"logged in one week"},{"value":"35 hrs","label":"in AI-friendly areas"},{"value":"~8 hrs","label":"realistic weekly target"}],"caption":"A modest, honest target: about one working day back each week."},
{"t":"p","text":"Why only about 8 hours out of 35? Because every AI-assisted step still needs a human review, and some steps cannot change at all. People who expect AI to halve their week usually end up disappointed and abandon the effort. A realistic target keeps you going."},
{"t":"check","q":"Elena’s log shows 6 hours of meetings. Why does she leave them out of the redesign, at least for now?","a":"Meetings are mostly live conversation, relationships and judgment. AI can help with an agenda or notes from her own summary, but the hours themselves do not shrink much. Starting with word-heavy, repetitive work gives bigger, safer wins."},
{"t":"exercise","title":"Log and decompose one of your tasks","prompt":"Choose one recurring task from your own week. Break it into 4 to 7 steps with an estimated time for each, then mark each step as AI-friendly (pattern or language work), human-only (judgment, relationships, sign-off) or mixed. Use Elena’s month-end accrual review for Client B as a model.","steps":["Write the task name and how often it happens.","List the steps in the order you actually do them.","Put a time estimate next to each step.","Label each step AI-friendly, human-only or mixed.","Circle the two steps with the biggest AI potential."],"answer":"Worked model: Client B month-end accrual review, monthly, about 95 minutes.\n\n1. Pull last month’s accrual list and this month’s open invoices (10 min): human-only, it needs system access.\n2. Compare which accruals should reverse and which are new (20 min): mixed. AI can write a formula that flags matches, but she decides.\n3. Estimate amounts for services received but not yet invoiced, such as utilities (20 min): human-only, it needs judgment and client knowledge.\n4. Write the support memo for each accrual over the firm’s threshold (25 min): AI-friendly. A template turns her bullet notes into clear support text.\n5. Draft questions for the client about unclear items (10 min): AI-friendly, using her email template.\n6. Final review and sign-off (10 min): human-only.\n\nBiggest AI potential: steps 4 and 5, about 35 minutes. A realistic saving is 15 to 20 minutes a month, because she still reads every word. That sounds small, but across four clients and twelve months it adds up to roughly 12 to 16 hours a year from one task."},
{"t":"chapter","title":"Sort the tasks: where AI fits and where it does not","minutes":7},
{"t":"p","text":"Elena now has about thirty steps across her five areas. She cannot redesign them all at once, and she should not. She needs a way to sort them, the same way she would sort audit risks: by likelihood of payoff and by consequence of failure."},
{"t":"h","text":"Four questions for every step"},
{"t":"list","ordered":true,"items":["**Repetition:** how often does it happen? Weekly beats yearly.","**Pattern or judgment:** could you write the rules on one page, or does it depend on experience and context?","**Data sensitivity:** what is the most sensitive information the step would need to show the AI?","**Cost of error:** if the AI is wrong and you miss it, what happens? A clumsy sentence, or a wrong number in a client’s filing?"]},
{"t":"p","text":"The first two questions measure _benefit_. The last two measure _risk_. A step that is frequent and pattern-based but involves restricted data might still be a great candidate, if you can redesign it so the AI never sees that data. You will see how in chapter 3."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"matrix","xAxis":"Judgment needed →","yAxis":"Repetition →","cells":[{"title":"Template + spot-check","note":"frequent, rule-based"},{"title":"AI drafts, you decide","note":"frequent, judgment-heavy"},{"title":"Quick one-off prompt","note":"rare, rule-based"},{"title":"Human first","note":"rare, judgment-heavy"}],"caption":"Sort each step by how often it repeats and how much judgment it needs."},
{"t":"p","text":"The top-left box is the gold mine: frequent and rule-based steps, like drafting reconciling-item explanations or status emails. Build a template once, use it every week, and spot-check. The top-right box is still valuable but needs more review: memos and review notes repeat, yet every one needs your judgment."},
{"t":"p","text":"The bottom-right box is where AI is a thinking partner at most. A one-time question about how to structure a complex intercompany arrangement for Client F deserves your full attention and real sources. AI can help you list questions, but it should not lead."},
{"t":"h","text":"Elena’s scoring table"},
{"t":"p","text":"She scores each area from 1 to 3 on fit (repetition plus pattern) and on risk (sensitivity plus error cost). Then she starts with high fit and low or medium risk."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"table","head":["Area","Hours","AI fit","Risk"],"rows":[["Reconciliations","9","High","Medium"],["Client emails","8","High","Low to medium"],["Reviewing juniors","7","Medium","Medium"],["Memos","6","Medium","High"],["Research","5","Medium","High"]],"caption":"Fit says where the hours are; risk says how much control to build."},
{"t":"p","text":"Notice that memos and research score **high risk** even though they are not about private data. The risk is different: an invented citation or a misremembered rule in a technical memo could mislead a client. Risk is not only about privacy. It is about any way a mistake could leave your desk."},
{"t":"warn","text":"Do not start with the riskiest task because it feels most impressive. Start where the payoff is clear and the mistakes are cheap, learn how AI behaves on your work, then move up."},
{"t":"h","text":"Three roles AI can play"},
{"t":"p","text":"For each step Elena keeps, she also decides what _role_ AI plays. This single choice shapes the prompt, the review and the risk."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"spectrum","left":"You do more","right":"AI does more","items":[{"label":"Sparring partner","pos":15},{"label":"Drafter","pos":50},{"label":"Builder of tools","pos":70},{"label":"Acts alone","pos":95}],"caption":"Elena uses the first three roles; acting alone is off limits for client work."},
{"t":"list","items":["**Sparring partner:** it asks questions, challenges your reasoning, lists what you might be missing. You write the answer.","**Drafter:** it writes a first version from your notes and template. You edit and own it.","**Builder of tools:** it writes a formula, a macro or a small script that you run on your own machine, so the data never needs to reach the AI.","**Acts alone:** it sends, posts or files with no human step. Elena does not allow this for anything client-facing."]},
{"t":"p","text":"The builder role is underrated. When AI writes a formula that runs in your spreadsheet, the AI only needed a description of the columns. The client’s numbers never left your computer. That is the most elegant privacy control of all: the AI never sees what it does not need."},
{"t":"exercise","title":"Score five steps","prompt":"Score these five steps from Elena’s week on fit (High, Medium, Low) and risk (High, Medium, Low), choose a role for AI, and say which two you would redesign first: (a) drafting the weekly status update to Client D’s controller; (b) deciding whether Client F’s intercompany charge needs a transfer pricing review; (c) building a formula to flag duplicate vendor invoices; (d) writing coaching notes for a junior after a review; (e) translating a routine English email into Italian for Client F’s parent company.","steps":["Ask the four questions for each step.","Place each step in the matrix.","Pick a role: sparring partner, drafter or builder of tools.","Choose two to start with and justify."],"answer":"Worked scoring:\n\n(a) Weekly status update: fit High (weekly, fixed structure), risk Low (her own notes, reviewed before sending). Role: drafter. Matrix: template + spot-check.\n\n(b) Transfer pricing question: fit Low (rare, deep judgment), risk High (tax exposure, possible penalties). Role: sparring partner only, to list questions and facts to gather. Matrix: human first. This goes to the partner and real sources.\n\n(c) Duplicate invoice formula: fit High (every close), risk Low to medium (the formula is tested on known rows, data stays local). Role: builder of tools. Matrix: template + spot-check.\n\n(d) Coaching notes: fit Medium (frequent but personal), risk Medium (tone matters, no client data needed if anonymized). Role: drafter from her rough notes. Matrix: AI drafts, you decide.\n\n(e) Routine translation: fit High (frequent with Client F), risk Medium (a wrong nuance can confuse a parent company). Role: drafter, and she reads every line because she understands Italian.\n\nStart with (c) and (a): both are frequent, low risk and easy to measure. They build confidence and free time to design the harder workflows carefully."},
{"t":"chapter","title":"Privacy by design: the control layer","minutes":8},
{"t":"p","text":"Before Elena redesigns a single workflow, she builds the layer that sits under all of them: data controls. An accountant’s value rests on trust. One careless paste of a client’s payroll file into the wrong tool can do more damage than a year of time savings is worth."},
{"t":"p","text":"The goal is not fear. It is design. Just as a good close process has controls built in, so nobody has to remember them under pressure, a good AI workflow makes the safe path the easy path."},
{"t":"h","text":"Classify the data"},
{"t":"p","text":"Elena uses four tiers, matching her firm’s information policy. Every workflow names the highest tier it touches, and that tier decides which tools and which steps are allowed."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"pyramid","levels":["Restricted: never","Confidential: approved tools","Internal: approved tools","Public: free to use"],"caption":"The higher the tier, the fewer the tools and the tighter the rules."},
{"t":"list","items":["**Restricted:** Social Security numbers, tax ID numbers, bank and card account numbers, passwords, login codes, health or immigration details. These never go into any AI prompt.","**Confidential:** client names linked to their financials, payroll by person, contracts, unpublished results. Only in tools the firm has approved for client data, and anonymized whenever the task allows it.","**Internal:** firm templates, checklists, training notes, process descriptions. Approved tools only.","**Public:** accounting standards, IRS publications, published laws, public company filings. Any tool your firm allows."]},
{"t":"p","text":"Why does even approved use matter? Because when you paste text into an AI tool, it is sent to a service provider and may be stored under that provider’s terms. Your firm’s IT or risk team knows what the firm’s agreement says about storage, retention and whether data may be used to train models. Do not assume. Ask, and keep their answer in writing."},
{"t":"term","word":"Pseudonymization","def":"Replacing real names and identifiers with neutral codes, like “Client F” or “Vendor 12”, while you keep the key that links codes to real names on your own secure system."},
{"t":"h","text":"The anonymization pipeline"},
{"t":"p","text":"For confidential data, Elena uses a simple pipeline. She keeps a small key table in an access-controlled file on the firm’s system: “Vendor 12 = the real supplier name”. The AI only ever sees codes. When the answer comes back, she puts the real names back in on her own machine."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"flow","steps":[{"label":"Raw data","icon":"🗂️","note":"stays inside"},{"label":"Strip and code","icon":"✂️","note":"names → codes"},{"label":"AI works","icon":"🤖","note":"sees codes only"},{"label":"Re-identify","icon":"🔑","note":"on your machine"},{"label":"Human review","icon":"✅","note":"before it leaves"}],"caption":"Pseudonymize on the way in, re-identify on the way out."},
{"t":"p","text":"Codes are not magic. A description like “only steel supplier in Ohio for a family-owned firm” can identify a client even without a name. So she also removes unique details that are not needed for the task, and rounds or scales numbers when the exact amounts do not matter, for example when she only needs help with the structure of an explanation."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"compare","left":{"title":"Paste the data","icon":"📋","items":["Real names and amounts","Account numbers inside","Easy now, risky later","Hard to undo"]},"right":{"title":"Paste the shape","icon":"📐","items":["Column layout only","Codes, not names","Sample rows invented","Nothing to leak"]},"caption":"For formulas and scripts, the AI needs the shape of the data, not the data."},
{"t":"h","text":"Elena’s seven controls"},
{"t":"list","ordered":true,"items":["**Approved tools only:** client data goes only into tools on the firm’s approved list, with work accounts, never personal ones.","**Minimum data:** share only what the step needs. Shape before sample, sample before full file.","**Codes, not names:** pseudonymize confidential data and keep the key on the firm’s system.","**No restricted data, ever:** no ID numbers, account numbers, passwords or login codes, even in approved tools.","**Draft, never send:** AI output is a draft until a human reviews it. Nothing leaves automatically.","**Log it:** for each workflow, a one-line record of the tool, the data tier and the reviewer.","**Clean up:** delete chats and uploaded files when the work is done, as policy allows."]},
{"t":"p","text":"Control 6, the log, is what turns personal habits into something you can show. If a partner or a client asks “how do you use AI on our file?”, Elena can open a one-page register and answer in a minute. You will build that register in chapter 8."},
{"t":"p","text":"A note on Client F. Its parent company is in Italy, and the parent sometimes sends employee data for payroll allocations. Personal data about people in the European Union falls under the EU’s data-protection law, the GDPR, which is stricter than most US rules about where personal data may be sent and why. Elena’s rule is simple: European employee data does not go near an AI tool unless the firm’s privacy lead has said yes in writing."},
{"t":"warn","text":"The most common leak is not a hacker. It is a tired professional at 7 p.m. pasting a whole spreadsheet “just this once” into a personal chatbot account. Design your workflows so that never feels necessary."},
{"t":"check","q":"Elena wants help writing a formula to split Client B’s payroll costs across three restaurant locations. What does the AI actually need to see?","a":"Only the layout: which columns hold the employee code, the location and the cost, the row range, and the rule for the split. She can describe it in words or with two or three invented sample rows. No real names, salaries or ID numbers are needed at all."},
{"t":"exercise","title":"Redact a request before it goes in","prompt":"Elena drafted this note to paste into an AI tool. Rewrite it so it is safe under her seven controls while still useful: “Help me write a reply to Marco Bellini, CFO of [real company] US Inc. He says our Q3 intercompany invoice of $187,430.55 to Bellini Group SpA is wrong because they paid it from account ending 4471. Their EIN is 12-3456789. He’s annoyed and wants an answer today.”","steps":["Find every restricted item and remove it.","Replace confidential names with codes.","Keep only the facts the reply needs.","Decide whether exact amounts are needed."],"answer":"What has to go:\n• Restricted: the account number fragment and the EIN. Neither is needed to write a reply. Remove both completely.\n• Confidential: the person’s name and both company names. Replace them with codes.\n• The exact amount: the AI does not need it to draft the structure and tone. Use a placeholder.\n\nSafe version to paste:\n“Context: I’m a senior accountant. The CFO of Client F, a US subsidiary, has written to say our quarterly intercompany invoice to its parent company is wrong. He believes it was already paid. He’s frustrated and wants a reply today. I haven’t yet confirmed whether he’s right.\nTask: Draft a short reply that thanks him, acknowledges the concern without admitting an error, explains that I’m checking the payment records now, and promises a full answer by [time] today.\nFormat: Under 120 words, calm and professional. Address him as [Name].\nLimits: Don’t state any amounts or account details. Use [AMOUNT] and [INVOICE #] as placeholders.”\n\nWhy it works: the AI has everything it needs for tone and structure, and nothing a leak could expose. Elena fills in the placeholders in her own email client, after checking the payment records herself."},
{"t":"chapter","title":"Workflow 1: reconciliations without pasting the books","minutes":8},
{"t":"p","text":"Reconciliations are Elena’s biggest block: 9 hours a week across four clients. They are also high stakes, because a reconciliation is a control. If it is wrong, errors in the books go unnoticed. So her design rule is strict: **AI may build the tools and draft the words; the numbers come from the spreadsheet and the sign-off comes from her.**"},
{"t":"fig","kind":"equation","parts":["Bank balance","±","Reconciling items","=","Book balance"],"caption":"The goal never changes: every difference explained and supported."},
{"t":"h","text":"The redesigned process"},
{"t":"fig","kind":"flow","steps":[{"label":"Describe layout","icon":"📐","note":"shape only"},{"label":"AI builds tool","icon":"🛠️","note":"formula or script"},{"label":"Run locally","icon":"💻","note":"data stays home"},{"label":"Test known rows","icon":"🧪","note":"prove it works"},{"label":"Review exceptions","icon":"🔍","note":"human judgment"}],"caption":"The AI builds the matching tool once; the books never leave the firm."},
{"t":"p","text":"In the workshop, Elena asked for one formula per reconciliation. Now she goes further. She asks the AI to build a **reusable matching workbook**: one sheet for the bank export, one for the general ledger, and a results sheet with clear flags. She builds it once, tests it, saves it as a firm template, and reuses it for every client and every month."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"chat","messages":[{"role":"you","text":"Context: Excel for Microsoft 365. I reconcile bank accounts monthly. Sheet “Bank”: A date, B description, C amount (deposits +, withdrawals −). Sheet “GL”: A date, B memo, C amount, same signs. Up to 2,000 rows each.\nTask: Design a results sheet that (1) matches one-to-one on exact amount within 5 days, never using a GL line twice, (2) lists unmatched items from both sides, (3) shows control totals.\nFormat: Step-by-step build instructions, then each formula with a plain explanation.\nCheck: Tell me where this design is weakest."},{"role":"ai","text":"Here’s a four-part design: a helper column that ranks repeated amounts so each GL line is used once, a match column, two unmatched lists, and a control panel showing row counts and totals for matched and unmatched items...\nWeakest points: one bank deposit that covers several GL entries won’t match; fees netted from deposits won’t match; dates more than 5 days apart need manual review."}],"caption":"A reusable tool, built from a description of the columns alone."},
{"t":"p","text":"Notice what the AI never saw: a single real transaction. It also volunteered its own weak points, because Elena asked. Those weak points become her review focus: batched deposits, netted fees and late postings are exactly where she looks first."},
{"t":"h","text":"The control panel"},
{"t":"p","text":"A tool you did not build line by line needs proof that it works every time it runs. Elena adds a small control panel to the template. If any check fails, she stops and investigates before reading a single match."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"table","head":["Control check","Must equal","Why"],"rows":[["Bank rows matched + unmatched","Total bank rows","No row lost"],["Matched bank total","Matched GL total","Pairs agree"],["Bank sheet total","Statement activity","Import complete"],["GL sheet total","Ledger cash activity","Import complete"],["5 known test rows","Expected results","Logic still works"]],"caption":"Five checks that prove the tool, every month, before any judgment begins."},
{"t":"p","text":"The last row matters most over time. Spreadsheets change: someone inserts a column, the bank changes its export format, a formula gets copied badly. Five rows where she already knows the answer will catch most of these silent failures in seconds."},
{"t":"h","text":"Explaining the reconciling items"},
{"t":"p","text":"After the matching, Client D’s October rec has twelve unmatched items. Elena investigates each one herself, using the client’s documents. Then she uses AI for the part that used to take 15 minutes of typing: turning her rough notes into clear, consistent explanations for the workpaper."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"chat","messages":[{"role":"you","text":"Turn my notes into reconciling-item explanations for a bank rec workpaper. One sentence each, past tense, professional. Keep my codes and amounts exactly as written. Don’t add causes I didn’t give.\n\n1) chk 1045 2,300 vendor 12 issued 10/29 not cleared\n2) dep 10/31 4,850 in transit, cleared 11/2\n3) bank fee 35 not booked, JE prepared"},{"role":"ai","text":"1. Check 1045 for $2,300 to Vendor 12 was issued on 10/29 and had not cleared the bank by month-end.\n2. A deposit of $4,850 recorded on 10/31 was in transit at month-end and cleared on 11/2.\n3. A $35 bank service fee was not recorded in the ledger at month-end; an adjusting entry has been prepared."}],"caption":"Her facts, her codes, her amounts: the AI only supplies clean wording."},
{"t":"p","text":"Is it safe to paste these notes? They use codes, not names. The amounts are confidential but not restricted, and the firm has approved this tool for client data. Elena logs the workflow at the confidential tier. If the firm had not approved any tool for client data, she would invent the amounts in the notes and replace them afterwards, or simply keep this step manual."},
{"t":"warn","text":"Never ask AI to add up, net or roll forward reconciliation amounts. Language models predict text, not arithmetic, and a total that looks right can be off by a digit. Totals come from the spreadsheet, always."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"bars","data":[{"label":"Before: matching","value":35,"highlight":false},{"label":"After: matching","value":10,"highlight":true},{"label":"Before: write-ups","value":15,"highlight":false},{"label":"After: write-ups","value":6,"highlight":true}],"unit":"","caption":"Illustrative minutes for one rec: big savings on the two AI-friendly steps."},
{"t":"p","text":"Across about five reconciliations a week, saving roughly 34 minutes each gives close to 3 hours back. The investigation and sign-off take as long as before, and they should. That is where her expertise lives."},
{"t":"exercise","title":"Design the credit card rec workflow","prompt":"Client B has three company credit cards with about 300 transactions a month. The card statement export has: transaction date, merchant, amount, cardholder code. The ledger has: date, vendor, amount, expense account. Design the AI-assisted workflow: write the tool-building prompt, list the control checks, and state which data tier each step touches.","steps":["Write a shape-only prompt to build the matching tool.","Ask for weak points.","Define at least four control checks.","Map each step to a data tier and a human review point."],"answer":"Tool-building prompt (shape only, nothing confidential):\n“Context: Excel for Microsoft 365. Sheet ‘Card’: A transaction date, B merchant, C amount (charges positive, refunds negative), D cardholder code (C1, C2, C3), up to 500 rows. Sheet ‘GL’: A date, B vendor, C amount, D expense account, up to 500 rows.\nTask: Build a results sheet that matches each card line to one GL line on exact amount within 4 days, never reusing a GL line; flags unmatched items on both sides; and summarizes unmatched totals by cardholder code.\nFormat: Build steps, then each formula with a one-line explanation.\nCheck: List the situations where this design will fail or mislead me.”\n\nWeak points to expect: refunds booked as negative expenses on a different date; merchant names that differ from vendor names; split transactions; foreign-currency charges where the amount changes slightly.\n\nControl checks:\n1. Card rows matched + unmatched = total card rows.\n2. Matched card total = matched GL total.\n3. Card sheet total = statement total charges minus credits.\n4. Unmatched totals by cardholder add back to the total unmatched.\n5. Four known test rows, including one refund, give the expected result.\n\nData tiers:\n• Building the tool: internal (layout only), any approved tool.\n• Running it: confidential, but local only; the AI sees nothing.\n• Investigating unmatched items: confidential, human only, with receipts.\n• Drafting explanations: confidential, approved tool, codes for cardholders and merchants.\n• Sign-off: human only, after the control checks pass.\n\nHuman review points: after the control panel (stop if anything fails), on every unmatched item, and on the final wording before it goes into the workpaper."},
{"t":"chapter","title":"Workflow 2: client emails with one voice","minutes":7},
{"t":"p","text":"Client email is Elena’s second biggest block, 8 hours a week. In the workshop she learned to write one good email prompt at a time. Now she designs a system so that every email sounds like her firm, takes less typing, and never carries a detail she did not intend."},
{"t":"h","text":"Standing instructions: write the briefing once"},
{"t":"p","text":"Most AI tools let you save instructions that apply to every chat in a project or workspace. You met this idea in the 201 lesson on context. Elena writes her firm’s email voice once, as standing instructions, so each new email prompt can be just a few lines of notes."},
{"t":"example","title":"Elena’s standing instructions for client email","text":"You help a senior accountant at a US CPA firm draft client emails.\nVoice: warm, clear, confident. Short sentences. No filler such as “I hope this finds you well”.\nStructure: one-line opening, the point, a numbered list for any requests, a clear next step with a date, a short sign-off.\nLength: under 150 words unless I say otherwise.\nFacts: use only facts in my notes. Never invent dates, amounts, fees, deadlines, tools or promises. If something seems missing, add a line at the end starting “NOTE TO ELENA:” instead of guessing.\nNames: use the codes I give (Client F, [Name]). Never ask me for real names, account numbers or ID numbers.\nTax and accounting rules: never state thresholds, rates or dates; write [CHECK]."},
{"t":"p","text":"Two lines in these instructions do most of the work. “Use only facts in my notes” stops invented promises. “NOTE TO ELENA” gives the AI a safe place to flag gaps, so it does not fill them with plausible fiction. Every draft now ends with either nothing or a short list of questions for her."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"compare","left":{"title":"Ad hoc prompts","icon":"🎲","items":["Retype context each time","Tone varies by mood","Rules forgotten when tired","Hard for others to reuse"]},"right":{"title":"Standing instructions","icon":"📌","items":["Context written once","One consistent voice","Rules apply every time","Shareable with the team"]},"caption":"Standing instructions turn good habits into defaults."},
{"t":"h","text":"From notes to draft in one minute"},
{"t":"fig","kind":"chat","messages":[{"role":"you","text":"Client D controller. Oct close done. 2 open items: vendor 7 invoice 9,200 missing PO; inventory count sheets from warehouse 2. Need both by Wed. Next: draft financials Fri."},{"role":"ai","text":"Subject: October close: two items to finish\n\nHi [Name],\n\nGood news: October’s close is complete apart from two items:\n\n1. The purchase order for Vendor 7’s $9,200 invoice\n2. The inventory count sheets from Warehouse 2\n\nIf we can have both by Wednesday, we’ll send draft financials on Friday.\n\nBest,\nElena\n\nNOTE TO ELENA: Which Wednesday? Add the date if helpful."}],"caption":"Rough notes in, a firm-voice draft out, with a flag where the notes were thin."},
{"t":"p","text":"The flag is the system working. Elena adds “November 5” and sends. Writing that email from scratch used to take her eight or ten minutes. Now it takes two, including a careful read."},
{"t":"h","text":"Triage without pasting the inbox"},
{"t":"p","text":"It is tempting to paste the whole inbox and ask “what should I answer first?” Elena does not, because a raw inbox mixes every client’s confidential details and sometimes restricted data in attachments. Instead she spends three minutes each morning writing her own one-line list, with codes, and asks the AI to help her plan."},
{"t":"p","text":"“Here are today’s 14 open emails as one-line notes with codes. Sort them into: answer before 10 a.m., answer today, can wait, delegate to a junior. Explain each choice in five words. I have meetings 11 to 1.” Her own summary is the privacy filter, and writing it already clarifies her thinking."},
{"t":"h","text":"Two languages, one standard"},
{"t":"p","text":"Client F’s parent company prefers Italian for anything formal. Elena reads Italian well, so she drafts in English and asks the AI for an Italian version, with one extra instruction: “Keep the accounting terms consistent with Italian practice, and list any term where the Italian and US meanings differ.”"},
{"t":"p","text":"That list is gold. For example, a word that looks like a direct translation of a US accounting term can carry a different technical meaning under Italian rules. The AI flags it, and Elena chooses the right word. Her rule: never send a translation she cannot fully read herself. When she writes to Client F’s Spanish-speaking logistics team, the same rule applies."},
{"t":"warn","text":"Translation hides invented details well, because you read it more slowly and trust the structure. Check numbers, dates and names in the translated version line by line against the original."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"donut","data":[{"label":"Routine updates","value":40},{"label":"Document requests","value":25},{"label":"Questions to answer","value":20},{"label":"Sensitive or new","value":15}],"center":"Email mix","caption":"Illustrative share of Elena’s emails: 85% fit a template; 15% she writes herself."},
{"t":"p","text":"The last slice is the most important design decision. Bad news, fee discussions, complaints and anything new get her full attention from the first word. She may still ask AI to critique her draft, but she writes it. A client can feel the difference."},
{"t":"exercise","title":"Write your standing instructions","prompt":"Write standing instructions for a different recurring workflow: the monthly management letter Elena sends to Client B’s owner, a one-page summary of the month’s results with three observations. The owner is not an accountant. Include voice, structure, length, facts rules, privacy rules and a flagging rule.","steps":["Describe who the AI is helping and who reads the output.","Set voice, structure and length.","Write the facts rule and the flagging rule.","Add privacy and numbers rules."],"answer":"Worked standing instructions:\n\n“You help a senior accountant at a US CPA firm draft a monthly one-page letter to the owner of a small restaurant group. The owner is smart and busy but not an accountant.\nVoice: plain English, warm, direct. Explain any accounting term in a few words the first time you use it. No jargon such as ‘accrual reversal’ without an explanation.\nStructure: (1) one-paragraph headline of the month, (2) three observations, each with what happened, why it matters and one suggested question or action, (3) what we need from you, (4) a one-line sign-off.\nLength: under 350 words.\nFacts: use only the figures and comments in my notes. Never calculate new totals, percentages or comparisons; if a comparison would help, write [CALC: what to compare] and I’ll compute it in the spreadsheet.\nFlagging: if my notes are unclear or seem inconsistent, add ‘NOTE TO ELENA:’ at the end with your question. Never guess.\nPrivacy: use location codes (Location 1, 2, 3) and role titles instead of employee names. Never ask for or include account numbers or ID numbers.\nAdvice: describe options and questions, never give legal or investment advice.”\n\nWhy it works: the [CALC] rule keeps all math in the spreadsheet, which is where it belongs. The structure gives the owner the same easy layout every month, and the flagging rule turns gaps into questions instead of invented facts."},
{"t":"chapter","title":"Workflow 3: memos and research you can defend","minutes":8},
{"t":"p","text":"Memos and research are only 11 hours of Elena’s week, but they carry the highest risk. A technical memo is read by partners and sometimes by auditors or regulators. If it relies on a rule that does not exist, or misstates one that does, the damage is real. So this workflow is built around one principle: **AI helps you think and write; primary sources decide what is true.**"},
{"t":"h","text":"The source ladder"},
{"t":"p","text":"Not all information is equally reliable. Elena pictures a ladder. The higher the rung, the more weight a statement can carry in a memo."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"spectrum","left":"Use for ideas","right":"Use as authority","items":[{"label":"AI from memory","pos":8},{"label":"AI with web links","pos":30},{"label":"Firm guidance","pos":55},{"label":"Research database","pos":72},{"label":"The standard itself","pos":95}],"caption":"AI helps you climb the ladder; only the top rungs belong in citations."},
{"t":"p","text":"An AI answer from memory is useful for vocabulary, for structure and for knowing which questions to ask. AI tools that search the web and show links move up a rung, but a link is a lead, not a citation: you still open it and read the actual words. The authority comes from the standard, the regulation, or your firm’s research tools."},
{"t":"h","text":"A memo workflow in five stages"},
{"t":"p","text":"The case: Client F signed a five-year lease for a new warehouse, with an option to extend for five more years. The question for the memo: should the extension be included in the lease term under ASC 842? The answer depends on whether the company is “reasonably certain” to exercise the option, which is a judgment based on facts."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"timeline","events":[{"when":"Stage 1","label":"You gather facts"},{"when":"Stage 2","label":"AI frames issues"},{"when":"Stage 3","label":"You read sources"},{"when":"Stage 4","label":"AI drafts to plan"},{"when":"Stage 5","label":"You verify, sign"}],"caption":"Stages 1, 3 and 5 are human; AI supports the thinking and the writing."},
{"t":"p","text":"**Stage 1, facts.** Elena writes the facts herself, from the lease and from conversations with Client F: the term, the option, the custom racking the company installed, the cost of moving, the location near its main customer. She uses codes and leaves out anything not needed."},
{"t":"p","text":"**Stage 2, framing.** She uses AI as a sparring partner. Her prompt asks for issues, not answers."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"chat","messages":[{"role":"you","text":"I’m drafting a technical memo under US GAAP. Facts: [my anonymized facts]. Question: should the 5-year extension option be in the lease term under ASC 842?\nDon’t give me a conclusion yet. Instead: (1) list the factors a preparer usually weighs for “reasonably certain”, (2) say which of my facts point each way, (3) list facts I should still gather. Don’t cite paragraph numbers; I’ll find them in the standard."},{"role":"ai","text":"Factors usually weighed: contract-based incentives, significant leasehold improvements, costs of relocating, importance of the asset to operations...\nPointing toward including the option: custom racking with a long useful life; high moving costs...\nPointing against: ...\nStill to gather: the expected useful life of the racking, the renewal rent compared with market, management’s plans..."}],"caption":"Ask for factors and gaps first; keep the conclusion for after the sources."},
{"t":"p","text":"**Stage 3, sources.** Now Elena reads the relevant sections of the standard in her firm’s research database, plus the firm’s internal guidance on lease term judgments. The AI’s list told her what to look for. The standard tells her what it actually says. She notes the paragraph references herself."},
{"t":"p","text":"**Stage 4, drafting.** She pastes her facts, her own summary of the guidance with references, and her tentative conclusion, and asks for a draft in the firm’s memo format: Background, Issue, Guidance, Analysis, Conclusion. She asks for a plan first, approves the outline, then asks for the full draft. The AI writes; it does not decide."},
{"t":"p","text":"**Stage 5, verification.** Elena checks every sentence that states a rule against her notes from the standard, and every fact against the lease. Then she asks the AI for one last service: “Act as a skeptical reviewer. List the three weakest points in this analysis.” Two of the three are fair, and she strengthens them before the partner sees it."},
{"t":"key","text":"Design every AI workflow so that judgment, facts and sign-off stay with you, and the AI does the parts where a mistake is easy to catch."},
{"t":"warn","text":"Invented citations are the classic AI failure in professional writing: a paragraph number, a ruling or a court case that looks perfect and does not exist. Rule: no citation enters a memo unless you have opened the source and read it yourself."},
{"t":"h","text":"Quick research questions"},
{"t":"p","text":"Not every question needs a memo. When a junior asks “do we capitalize this software implementation cost?”, Elena uses AI to get oriented in two minutes: the main concepts, the right vocabulary, the questions to ask. Then she checks the answer in the firm’s research tool before replying. The AI made her faster at finding the right page. It did not replace the page."},
{"t":"exercise","title":"Plan a defensible memo","prompt":"Client D bought a small competitor and must decide whether a list of customer relationships should be recognized as a separate intangible asset. Write (a) the framing prompt you would use in Stage 2, and (b) your Stage 5 verification checklist. Use codes and no confidential amounts.","steps":["Write anonymized facts in two or three sentences.","Ask for factors, the direction of each fact and missing facts, not a conclusion.","Forbid citations in the framing stage.","List at least five verification checks for the final draft."],"answer":"(a) Framing prompt:\n“Context: I’m a senior accountant drafting a technical memo under US GAAP. Facts: Client D, a wholesale distributor, acquired Target Co, a smaller regional distributor. Target Co has about 200 active business customers, many with multi-year supply agreements, and a customer list kept in its sales system.\nTask: Help me frame the issue of whether customer-related intangible assets should be recognized separately from goodwill in this business combination.\nPlease: (1) list the criteria a preparer usually considers for recognizing an intangible separately from goodwill, (2) say which of my facts point toward or against each, (3) list facts I still need, such as contract terms, renewal history and how valuation specialists typically approach this asset.\nLimits: Don’t reach a conclusion. Don’t cite paragraph numbers or specific guidance by number; I’ll find the guidance myself in our research database.”\n\n(b) Stage 5 verification checklist:\n1. Every rule stated in the memo matches my own notes from the standard, with references I looked up myself.\n2. Every fact matches the purchase agreement and client documents.\n3. No citation, paragraph number or example appears that I have not opened and read.\n4. The conclusion follows from the analysis; I can explain it out loud in two minutes without the memo.\n5. No amounts were calculated by the AI; all values come from the valuation report or the spreadsheet.\n6. A skeptical-reviewer pass was run, and each weakness raised was either fixed or answered in the memo.\n7. Codes are replaced with real names only in the final version on the firm’s system."},
{"t":"chapter","title":"Workflow 4: reviewing juniors’ work with AI","minutes":6},
{"t":"p","text":"Reviewing J., M. and S. takes Elena 7 hours a week. Much of that time goes to the same small problems: a missing tickmark, an unexplained variance, a reconciliation that ties but has stale items. AI cannot replace her review, which is a professional responsibility. But it can make the work arrive cleaner, and make her feedback clearer."},
{"t":"h","text":"Move the first review to the preparer"},
{"t":"p","text":"Elena builds a **self-review prompt** for her juniors. Before submitting a workpaper, a junior pastes their own anonymized summary of what they did and runs it against a checklist. The AI asks them questions. It never sees the full client file, and it never approves anything."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"cycle","steps":["Junior prepares","AI self-review","Junior fixes","Elena reviews","Coaching note"],"center":"Review loop","caption":"The AI catches the easy issues first, so Elena’s review time goes to judgment."},
{"t":"example","title":"The self-review prompt (shared with juniors)","text":"You are a careful reviewer at a US CPA firm. I’m a junior accountant about to submit a [workpaper type]. Below is my summary of the work, with codes instead of names.\nCheck my summary against this list and ask me one question for each item that seems missing or unclear:\n1. Does the balance tie to the trial balance, and did I say how?\n2. Is every reconciling item explained, dated and supported?\n3. Are any items older than 60 days, and did I explain why?\n4. Did I note who prepared the work, the date and the sources used?\n5. Is there anything unusual compared with last month that I didn’t mention?\nDon’t rewrite my work. Don’t say it is approved. Just ask questions."},
{"t":"p","text":"Two lines protect the process: “Don’t rewrite my work” keeps the learning with the junior, and “Don’t say it is approved” keeps authority with the reviewer. After a month, Elena counts her review notes. They dropped from about 14 per workpaper to about 6, and the remaining notes are about judgment, not missing basics."},
{"t":"h","text":"Clearer coaching notes"},
{"t":"p","text":"Elena’s own review notes used to be blunt shorthand: “stale items??”, “support”. Juniors guessed at what she meant. Now she types her shorthand and asks the AI to turn it into notes that explain the issue, why it matters and what good looks like, in a respectful tone, without changing the substance."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"chat","messages":[{"role":"you","text":"Turn my review shorthand into coaching notes for a junior, about a year in. Kind, specific, short. Keep my meaning exactly; don’t add new issues.\n1) stale items?? 3 checks >90d\n2) JE support missing for 4,100 reclass"},{"role":"ai","text":"1. Three outstanding checks are more than 90 days old. Please find out whether they were voided, reissued or are still valid, and note what you learned. Old items can hide errors or unclaimed funds.\n2. The $4,100 reclassification entry needs support attached, such as the invoice or an email approving it, so a reviewer can see why it was booked."}],"caption":"Shorthand in, teachable notes out: same substance, better learning."},
{"t":"p","text":"Every few months, Elena also pastes her anonymized review notes, with no client names, and asks: “Group these into themes and suggest one 20-minute training topic for each.” The pattern is clear: stale items and missing support make up half the notes. That becomes next month’s team lunch session."},
{"t":"h","text":"When AI starts to act"},
{"t":"p","text":"Some AI tools can now take actions: read files, fill spreadsheets, draft entries in accounting software, send emails. These are often called **agents**. They can be useful, but they change the risk, because an action can happen before anyone reads it. Elena’s firm sets one clear rule, and she applies it to her juniors too."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"grid","items":[{"icon":"👀","label":"Read","note":"approved data only"},{"icon":"✍️","label":"Draft","note":"always allowed"},{"icon":"✋","label":"Act with approval","note":"a human clicks yes"},{"icon":"⛔","label":"Act alone","note":"not on client work"}],"caption":"Four permission levels for AI tools; client work stops at “act with approval”."},
{"t":"p","text":"In practice: an AI tool may prepare a draft journal entry, but a person posts it. It may prepare an email, but a person sends it. It may fill a workpaper, but a person reviews and signs. The more an AI can do on its own, the more important the human checkpoint becomes."},
{"t":"exercise","title":"Build a self-review prompt for accruals","prompt":"Write a self-review prompt that M., a junior, can run before submitting month-end accrual entries for Client B. Include at least five checklist items and the rules that protect the review process.","steps":["Set the AI’s role and the junior’s situation.","Write five or more checklist items specific to accruals.","Tell the AI how to respond: questions, not rewrites.","Add privacy and authority limits."],"answer":"Worked self-review prompt:\n\n“You are a careful reviewer at a US CPA firm. I’m a junior accountant about to submit month-end accrual entries for a restaurant client. Below is my summary of each accrual, using codes for vendors and locations.\nCheck my summary against this list. For each item that seems missing, unclear or inconsistent, ask me one specific question:\n1. Did last month’s accruals reverse, and did I confirm which have now been invoiced?\n2. For each new accrual, did I state the basis for the amount (contract, last invoice, usage estimate)?\n3. Is each accrual for goods or services actually received by month-end?\n4. Is each entry coded to a sensible expense account and location?\n5. Are any accruals unusually large or small compared with last month, and did I explain why?\n6. Did I attach or reference support for every accrual above the firm’s review threshold of [THRESHOLD]?\n7. Did I note preparer, date and sources?\nRules: Don’t rewrite my entries or calculate amounts. Don’t say the work is approved or correct; only my reviewer can do that. If I include any real names, account numbers or ID numbers, tell me to remove them before continuing.”\n\nWhy it works: the checklist is specific to accruals, so the questions are useful rather than generic. The rules keep the learning and the arithmetic with the junior, the approval with the reviewer, and add a privacy safety net in case someone pastes too much."},
{"t":"chapter","title":"Measure, govern and build your prompt library","minutes":10},
{"t":"p","text":"Eight weeks into the redesign, Elena faces a question from the managing partner: “Is this actually saving time, and is it safe?” Feelings are not an answer. Measurements and a written register are. This final chapter turns her experiments into a system she can defend and share."},
{"t":"h","text":"Measure the right thing"},
{"t":"p","text":"The trap is to measure only how fast the AI produces a draft. What matters is the full cycle, including your review and any fixes. Elena uses one simple formula for each workflow."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"equation","parts":["Net saved","=","Old time","−","New time","−","Review time"],"caption":"Count the review, or you will overstate the savings."},
{"t":"p","text":"She also tracks two quality measures, because time saved means nothing if quality drops: the number of corrections she makes to each AI draft, and the number of review notes per junior workpaper. If corrections stay high for a workflow, the template needs work, or the task does not suit AI."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"line","series":[{"name":"Hours on 5 areas","points":[35,34,32,31,29.5,28.5,27.5,27]},{"name":"Review hours inside","points":[0,1.5,2.5,2.5,2.5,2.2,2,2]}],"xLabels":["W0","W1","W2","W3","W4","W5","W6","W7"],"unit":"","caption":"Illustrative: total hours fall by 8 while review time settles at about 2 hours."},
{"t":"p","text":"Notice the shape. In week 1 the savings are small, because she is building templates and checking everything twice. Review time rises at first, then settles as the templates improve. By week 7 the five areas take 27 hours instead of 35: the day a week she aimed for. Her numbers are her own; yours will differ, and that is the point of measuring."},
{"t":"h","text":"The prompt library"},
{"t":"p","text":"Elena now has about twenty templates. A pile of twenty prompts in random notes is not a library; it is a drawer. She organizes them like any controlled document at the firm: each one has a name, a purpose, an owner, a version, a data tier and a date it was last tested."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"table","head":["Template","Use","Tier","Tested"],"rows":[["REC-01 Match builder","Build rec workbook","Internal","Oct, v3"],["REC-02 Item write-up","Explain rec items","Confidential","Oct, v2"],["EML-01 Firm voice","Client email drafts","Confidential","Sep, v4"],["MEM-01 Issue framing","Memo stage 2","Internal","Oct, v1"],["REV-01 Self-review","Junior pre-review","Confidential","Oct, v2"]],"caption":"A library entry has a code, a purpose, a data tier and a test date."},
{"t":"list","items":["**Codes by area:** REC, EML, MEM, RES, REV make templates easy to find on a phone.","**Versions:** when she improves a template, the number goes up and a one-line note says what changed.","**Test date:** every quarter she reruns each template on a known example, because tools and models change.","**Tier:** the tier tells anyone using it which tools are allowed.","**Blanks, never data:** templates contain [brackets], never real client details."]},
{"t":"p","text":"Why retest? AI tools are updated often, and a template that worked perfectly in spring can behave differently in autumn: longer answers, a new habit of adding details, different formatting. A quarterly test on a known example takes ten minutes and catches drift before a client sees it."},
{"t":"h","text":"The risk register"},
{"t":"p","text":"The second document is a one-page AI register, the control log from chapter 3 grown up. For each workflow it lists the main risk and the control that answers it. When the managing partner asks “is it safe?”, Elena shows this page."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"table","head":["Risk","Control"],"rows":[["Client data leaks","Approved tools, codes, no restricted data"],["Wrong number","All math in spreadsheet, control checks"],["Invented citation","Read every source yourself"],["Invented promise","“Only my facts” rule, read every line"],["Silent tool change","Quarterly retest of templates"],["Over-trust by juniors","No-approval rule, human sign-off"]],"caption":"Every risk has a named control; no control, no workflow."},
{"t":"p","text":"Accountability does not move. Professional standards, the firm’s policies and the client’s trust all point to the same place: the person who signs. AI changes how the work is produced. It does not change who answers for it. Elena writes that sentence at the top of her register."},
{"t":"h","text":"Sharing with the team"},
{"t":"p","text":"With measurements and a register in hand, Elena proposes a small pilot: her three juniors adopt REV-01 and EML-01 for one month, with a 30-minute training session on the seven privacy controls first. The partner agrees, on one condition: the register gets reviewed by the firm’s IT and risk lead before the pilot starts. That is exactly the right condition."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"timeline","events":[{"when":"Week 1","label":"Log your week"},{"when":"Week 2","label":"Classify data, set controls"},{"when":"Week 3","label":"Build 2 low-risk workflows"},{"when":"Week 4","label":"Measure, fix templates"},{"when":"Month 2","label":"Add higher-risk workflows"},{"when":"Month 3","label":"Register, retest, share"}],"caption":"A 90-day rollout: start small, measure, then widen."},
{"t":"check","q":"A colleague says her AI drafts save 30 minutes a day, but she spends 25 minutes a day fixing them. What does the net-saved formula say, and what should she do?","a":"Net saving is only about 5 minutes a day, and her correction count is high. She should improve the templates, for example with standing instructions and an “only my facts” rule, or move those tasks back to manual if they do not suit AI."},
{"t":"exercise","title":"Your library card and measurement plan","prompt":"Create a complete library entry for one template you will use, plus a four-week measurement plan. As a model, build the entry for Elena’s client status update template (EML-02), including the full prompt text.","steps":["Give the template a code, name, purpose, tier and owner.","Write the full prompt with brackets.","Define a known test example for quarterly retests.","Set a baseline and the measures you will track for four weeks."],"answer":"Library entry\nCode: EML-02 Weekly status update\nPurpose: turn rough weekly notes into a client status email\nTier: Confidential (codes only; approved tools only)\nOwner: Elena · Version: v1 · Last tested: [date]\n\nFull prompt:\n“Using the firm email voice in the standing instructions, turn my notes into this week’s status update for the controller of [client code], who prefers [bullets / short paragraphs].\nHeadings: Done this week · In progress · Waiting on you · Next week.\nFormat: under [150] words, headings in bold, one-line greeting and sign-off.\nLimits: use only facts in my notes; no new dates, amounts or promises; anything that fits no heading goes under ‘Other’ with a NOTE TO ELENA.\nMy notes: [anonymized notes]”\n\nQuarterly test example: a saved set of eight fake notes that includes one item with no clear heading and one missing date. A passing result puts every item under the right heading, adds nothing new, and flags both problems with NOTE TO ELENA.\n\nFour-week measurement plan:\n• Baseline (week 0): time three status emails written by hand. Say 12 minutes each.\n• Weeks 1 to 4: for every status email, record drafting time, review time and the number of corrections.\n• Targets: net time under 5 minutes per email by week 4; corrections at 1 or fewer per email by week 3.\n• Decision at week 4: if targets are met, keep the template and share it; if corrections stay high, revise the prompt (likely the ‘only my facts’ rule or the headings) and test again; if net time stays above 9 minutes, retire it."},
{"t":"h","text":"The week, redesigned"},
{"t":"p","text":"Look back at where Elena started: 50 hours, good prompts used at random, and a vague worry about privacy. Now she has four designed workflows, seven privacy controls, a twenty-template library, a risk register and numbers to prove the result. She got back about a day a week, and she spends part of it on the work AI cannot do: harder client questions, better coaching, and preparing for the CPA exam."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"pyramid","levels":["Measure and improve","Prompt library","Four workflows","Privacy controls"],"caption":"Build from the bottom: controls first, measurement last, all four layers together."},
{"t":"p","text":"Your week is different from Elena’s, but the method travels: log the week, break tasks into steps, sort by fit and risk, build the control layer, design a few workflows, measure honestly, and keep a library you can defend. Start on Monday with the log. In ninety days, you will have your own system."}
],
"takeaways":[
"Redesign steps, not whole tasks: AI takes pattern and language work, you keep judgment and sign-off.",
"Build privacy controls first: approved tools, minimum data, codes for names, no restricted data ever.",
"Let AI build tools that run locally, so client numbers never need to reach the AI.",
"Measure net time saved including review, and track corrections to judge each template.",
"Keep a versioned prompt library and a risk register; accountability always stays with the signer."
],
"quiz":[
{"q":"Elena logs 35 hours a week in AI-friendly areas but sets a target of about 8 hours saved. Why so modest?","options":["AI tools can only be used for a few hours a week","Every AI-assisted step still needs human review, and many steps cannot change","Her firm limits AI use to 8 hours","Savings above 8 hours are not allowed under professional standards"],"answer":1,"why":"AI replaces specific steps, not whole tasks, and review time is part of the cost. A realistic target keeps the redesign going."},
{"q":"Which step belongs in the “template + spot-check” box of the sorting matrix?","options":["Deciding whether a transfer pricing review is needed","Writing a one-time memo on a new type of transaction","Drafting the weekly status update from rough notes","Negotiating a fee increase with a client"],"answer":2,"why":"Status updates repeat weekly and follow fixed rules, so a template with a quick review fits perfectly."},
{"q":"Under Elena’s data tiers, where does a client’s bank account number belong?","options":["Public: free to use","Internal: approved tools","Confidential: approved tools, anonymized","Restricted: never in any AI prompt"],"answer":3,"why":"Account numbers, ID numbers and passwords are restricted. They never go into an AI tool, even an approved one."},
{"q":"Why is asking AI to build a matching formula from a column description such a strong privacy control?","options":["The AI only sees the layout, while the client’s numbers stay on your computer","Formulas are encrypted automatically","Spreadsheets cannot be read by AI tools","It makes the reconciliation exempt from review"],"answer":0,"why":"In the builder role the AI needs only the shape of the data. The real transactions never leave the firm."},
{"q":"Elena’s reconciliation tool shows matched bank total $81,200 and matched GL total $80,900. What should she do first?","options":["Ask the AI to explain the difference","Stop and investigate the tool and the data before reviewing any matches","Accept it, since the difference is small","Adjust the GL by $300"],"answer":1,"why":"Matched totals must agree. A failed control check means the tool or the import is wrong, so nothing else can be trusted until it is fixed."},
{"q":"What is the main purpose of the “NOTE TO ELENA” line in her standing instructions?","options":["To make emails longer","To sign the email automatically","To give the AI a safe place to flag gaps instead of inventing facts","To translate the email into Italian"],"answer":2,"why":"When the AI can flag a missing date or detail, it has no reason to fill the gap with a plausible invention."},
{"q":"On the source ladder, how should an AI tool’s web link be treated in a technical memo?","options":["As a lead: open it and read the actual source before relying on it","As an authoritative citation","As more reliable than the standard itself","As something to ignore completely"],"answer":0,"why":"Links move AI up a rung, but authority comes only from the source you have opened and read yourself."},
{"q":"In the memo workflow, which stages stay human?","options":["Only the final signature","Framing the issues and drafting","Gathering facts, reading the sources, and verifying and signing","None, if the AI tool is approved"],"answer":2,"why":"Facts, sources and sign-off carry the responsibility. AI supports framing and drafting, where mistakes are easier to catch."},
{"q":"Why does the juniors’ self-review prompt say “Don’t rewrite my work” and “Don’t say it is approved”?","options":["To make the AI answer faster","To keep the learning with the junior and the authority with the reviewer","Because AI cannot rewrite workpapers","To avoid using too many words"],"answer":1,"why":"The AI asks questions so juniors learn to find issues themselves, and only a human reviewer can approve work."},
{"q":"A template saves 20 minutes of drafting but adds 8 minutes of review and 7 minutes of fixes. What is the net saving per use?","options":["20 minutes","13 minutes","12 minutes","5 minutes"],"answer":3,"why":"Net saved = old time minus new time minus review. Here 20 − 8 − 7 = 5 minutes, and the high fix time suggests the template needs work."}
]
}
]});
